            <ArticleTitle>Effects of chronic treatment with phenazone on the hot-plate test and [3H]serotonin binding sites in pons and cortex membranes of the rat.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>84-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Many reports indicate that nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs exert their antinociceptive effect through adrenergic and serotoninergic systems. We investigated the acute and chronic effects of phenazone on the pain threshold and on brain serotonin binding sites. A relationship between phenazone serum levels and the antinociceptive effect was found; acute treatment with phenazone provokes a significant decrease in serotonin binding sites both in the pons and cerebral cortex after 2, 4 and 8 h, but not after 24 h. After 15 and 30 days of treatment, the number of binding sites increases both in the pons and cortex.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
